{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9be2e05a0167461a","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-9003\n3\tscore: 14\n4\tpass: true\n5\trecommendation: accept\n6\tissues: []\n7\terror: null\n8\t---\n9\t## Review Summary\n10\t\n11\tScore: 14/14 — pass\n12\tRecommendation: accept\n13\t\n14\t## Criterion Details\n15\t\n16\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n17\tBoth HLRs map to the single epic RHAISTRAT-9003-E001: HLR-1 (P1, 8-hour default + upgrade preservation) and HLR-2 (P2, release note and cluster-settings documentation). The traceability matrix is present in the decomposition summary and the epic carries a matching \"HLR Traceability\" section. Priority inheritance is correct: the epic is P1, the highest HLR priority. HLR-2 (P2) is bundled with the P1 work, but this is a triaged (`below-threshold`) strategy where the single-epic output is correct by design — priority collapse is intentionally permitted and is not flagged per the triage exception.\n18\t\n19\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n20\tTriage consistency check passes: `triage: below-threshold` with `epic_count: 1` — the single-epic output is correct for a triaged strategy. The DAG is a single node with no edges; the epic frontmatter has `dependencies: []`, matching the diagram and the summary. Critical path length is 1, within the S-size heuristic (1-2). No edges exist to be unjustified, no cross-epic serialization is possible, and no data/artifact dependency is uncaptured (the Dashboard validation is an acceptance criterion on the single epic, not a missing edge).\n21\t\n22\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n23\tOne epic, one component (Notebooks), one team (Workbenches) — matching the architecture context (Notebooks is owned by the Workbenches team; the shipped culler default lives in the notebook-controller midstream fork). The work is well under two weeks. The Dashboard is correctly not a separate epic: architecture context confirms culling is administere